            {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "name": "Full-response SSRF in workflow api_call action node (no URL validation, redirects followed)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Full-response SSRF in workflow api_call action node (no URL validation, redirects followed)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The workflow engine's api_call action node fetches an arbitrary attacker-supplied URL with no scheme, host, or IP validation, and returns the full response body to the caller. The fetch client follows redirects, allowing allowlist-free pivoting to loopback/link-internal targets. A related sink exists in the MCP server \"test connection\" endpoint, which HEADs an attacker-registered host_address with no validation (blind)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The workflow engine's api_call action node fetches an arbitrary attacker-supplied URL with no scheme, host, or IP validation, and returns the full response body to the caller. The fetch client follows redirects, allowing allowlist-free pivoting to loopback/link-internal targets. A related sink exists in the MCP server \"test connection\" endpoint, which HEADs an attacker-registered host_address with no validation (blind).\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege user who can create/edit a workflow (api_call node) makes the backend issue requests to arbitrary internal addresses (loopback, RFC1918, link-local/cloud metadata) and reads the full response body back through workflow execution results/logs — full (non-blind) SSRF. Redirect-following defeats any future allowlist unless re-validated per hop.\n\nIntroduce a shared SSRF guard for all outbound URL fetches driven by user input: validate scheme (https/http only), resolve DNS and reject loopback, RFC1918, link-local (169.254/16, metadata ranges), and IPv6-mapped variants; use `redirect: 'manual'` and re-validate each hop before following (or disable redirects entirely); cap response size. Apply the guard in the workflow api_call executors, the MCP test-connection path, and any future outbound fetch of user-supplied URLs.",
                "markdown": "The workflow engine's api_call action node fetches an arbitrary attacker-supplied URL with no scheme, host, or IP validation, and returns the full response body to the caller. The fetch client follows redirects, allowing allowlist-free pivoting to loopback/link-internal targets. A related sink exists in the MCP server \"test connection\" endpoint, which HEADs an attacker-registered host_address with no validation (blind).\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege user who can create/edit a workflow (api_call node) makes the backend issue requests to arbitrary internal addresses (loopback, RFC1918, link-local/cloud metadata) and reads the full response body back through workflow execution results/logs — full (non-blind) SSRF. Redirect-following defeats any future allowlist unless re-validated per hop.\n\nIntroduce a shared SSRF guard for all outbound URL fetches driven by user input: validate scheme (https/http only), resolve DNS and reject loopback, RFC1918, link-local (169.254/16, metadata ranges), and IPv6-mapped variants; use `redirect: 'manual'` and re-validate each hop before following (or disable redirects entirely); cap response size. Apply the guard in the workflow api_call executors, the MCP test-connection path, and any future outbound fetch of user-supplied URLs."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-306",
              "name": "Unauthenticated generic IM webhook allows arbitrary agent message injection and workflow triggering",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated generic IM webhook allows arbitrary agent message injection and workflow triggering"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The generic IM webhook endpoint `POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId` performs no authentication, signature verification, or replay protection. Any network caller who knows a binding UUID can inject arbitrary text into the agent chat pipeline, trigger agent execution, and create chat sessions."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The generic IM webhook endpoint `POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId` performs no authentication, signature verification, or replay protection. Any network caller who knows a binding UUID can inject arbitrary text into the agent chat pipeline, trigger agent execution, and create chat sessions.\n\nUnauthenticated remote attacker can drive the platform's AI agents with arbitrary prompts under a tenant's identity: create chat sessions (confirmed), inject persistent user messages, trigger agent/LLM execution and downstream agent actions, and consume tenant quota.\n\nRequire a per-binding authentication secret on the generic webhook endpoint (shared-secret header compared timing-safely, or HMAC over the raw body), validated before imService.handleMessage is invoked. Never allow a binding lookup path that bypasses adapter verifyRequest.",
                "markdown": "The generic IM webhook endpoint `POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId` performs no authentication, signature verification, or replay protection. Any network caller who knows a binding UUID can inject arbitrary text into the agent chat pipeline, trigger agent execution, and create chat sessions.\n\nUnauthenticated remote attacker can drive the platform's AI agents with arbitrary prompts under a tenant's identity: create chat sessions (confirmed), inject persistent user messages, trigger agent/LLM execution and downstream agent actions, and consume tenant quota.\n\nRequire a per-binding authentication secret on the generic webhook endpoint (shared-secret header compared timing-safely, or HMAC over the raw body), validated before imService.handleMessage is invoked. Never allow a binding lookup path that bypasses adapter verifyRequest."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
            },
